{
  "content": "{\"summary\":\"This lecture introduces computation through Python fundamentals: Python programs are sequences of definitions and commands that manipulate objects, expressions evaluate to values, and assignment binds names to values in memory. It contrasts natural-language ambiguity with a program's single execution meaning, surveys object types, operators, printing, type conversion, and variable rebinding, and uses errors and the radius/area example to show that the computer does only what it is told.\",\"key_points\":[\"In English, a syntactically correct phrase can have more than one meaning - for example, 'flying planes can be dangerous' can mean the act of flying a plane is dangerous or the plane in the air is dangerous, and 'This reading lamp hasn't uttered a word since I bought it' plays on 'reading lamp' - but a program has only one meaning; that meaning may still differ from the programmer's intent, leading to syntax errors (caught easily by Python, which points to the line), static semantic errors (caught if the program goes down the branch where the error occurs), unexpected output (for example, expecting 0 for a test case but getting 10), a crash, or a program that will not stop, with debugging covered later.\",\"Python programs are sequences of definitions and commands; expressions are evaluated and commands tell the interpreter to do something. In the shell you can type simple commands directly, such as 2 plus 4, for testing, while in the editor you write more lines and more complicated programs.\",\"In Python, everything is an object, and programs manipulate data objects. All objects have a type, and the type tells Python the kinds of operations allowed. There are two types of objects: scalar objects, which are basic and cannot be subdivided (for example, the number 5), and non-scalar objects, which have internal structure and can be subdivided (for example, the list 5, 6, 7, 8).\",\"Scalar objects in Python include integers (whole numbers), floats (real numbers, anything with a decimal), bools (Booleans with only two values, True and False, capital T and capital F), and NoneType (which has only one value, None, and represents the absence of a type). The type command, with the object in parentheses, finds an object's type; you can convert types by putting the target type before the object, so float(3) converts the integer 3 to the float 3.0, and converting any float to an integer truncates the decimal - it does not round.\",\"Printing is how you interact with the user and uses the print command. Typing 3 plus 2 in the shell shows the value 5, but that is just peeking at the value for the programmer, not actually printing it; typing 3 plus 2 in the editor and running the program prints nothing, whereas using the print statement prints the number 5 to the console. If there is no 'Out,' it was printed to the console.\",\"Expressions combine objects and operators and evaluate to a value; their syntax is object, operator, object. For ints and floats, +, -, and * give an integer result if both operands are integers but a float if at least one operand is a float; division always gives a float no matter the operands. The percent sign gives the remainder when dividing i by j, and i ** j raises i to the power of j. These operations follow typical mathematical precedence, and parentheses can change precedence.\",\"The equal sign is assignment, assigning the right-hand-side value to the left-hand-side variable name; this binds the name to the value in memory, and the name can later be used to refer to that value. Names should be descriptive and let you reuse names instead of values, making code nicer; for example, calculating the area of a circle uses pi and radius variables and evaluates an expression referring to them. In programming versus math, the right-hand side is always an expression evaluated to a value and the left-hand side is always a variable; to solve for x, you must tell the computer exactly how.\",\"Variable names can be rebound with new assignment statements. In the memory example, pi = 3.14, radius = 2.2, and area is computed; then radius = radius + 1 rebinds radius from 2.2 to 3.2, leaving 2.2 in memory with no handle (possibly reclaimed by the garbage collector), and radius points to the new value. The value of area did not change because the computer only does what it is told; it would change only if the instruction to recalculate area were also copied/executed.\"]}",
